     For the normal configuration, nethack doesn't do any escape
sequence manipulation itself, it just retrieves and sends the
relevant termcap or terminfo values specified for your terminal.
Presumeably the init or erase-screen entries are supplying those
codes.  NetHack doesn't know or care that they're there, and if
they don't actually work for some reason, that's a system problem
with the terminal database, not--at least not directly--a nethack
one.  What type of system are you using, and what does it think
you've got for a terminal?

     (At least one of the microcomputer ports supports compiling
to use hardcoded escape sequences rather than to obtain them
dynamically, but I'm sure that that has nothing to do with what
you're seeing.)

     What you're trying to accomplish is actually pretty similar
to trying to show tiles across a telnet connection and also to
using a robot to play the game:  interpreting and/or transforming
what the program is displaying.  Lots of folks have worked on
such things, with varying degrees of success.  Perhaps you should
investigate what they've done instead of starting from scratch.
I think parsing escape sequences to track what the screen looks
like is considered to be a last resort approach.


